{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: When history shows a sharp price spike (e.g., >3 EUR in 2 days) and sentiment remains neutral (0.00), the model fails to capture the breakout. Apply a positive bias adjustment of +3 to +5 EUR for the first 5-10 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "Adjust for excessive mean reversion in volatile regimes: The model assumes a slow, linear drift toward the recent mean. In reality, EU ETS prices exhibit strong momentum persistence after spikes. Reduce the model's downward slope or flat-line bias by 50% during the initial forecast window when recent history is trending up.",
    "Incorporate sentiment lag: Positive sentiment spikes (e.g., 0.50) often precede price increases by 1-3 days. If sentiment history shows a spike but the model forecast is flat or declining, override the model with a short-term upward correction.",
    "Account for post-spike volatility: After a major rally, the model underestimates the depth of subsequent corrections or the speed of recovery. Do not rely on the model's smooth trajectory; expect higher variance and potential overshoots in both directions during the 10-20 day horizon.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: The model consistently underestimates the peak price in bull runs (e.g., predicting ~79 EUR when true peak is ~89 EUR). For any forecast starting below 80 EUR with an upward trend in history, add a +8 to +10 EUR buffer to the 15-day peak projection."
  ]
}
